## FINDINGS (observations for the backlog — not FAIL, none in scope of the
## attempt-2 criteria)

F-Z5-3 (doc drift, cosmetic). internal/handlers/whatif/spending_trajectory_test.go,
in attempt-1's TestBuildSpendingTrajectoryRows_FallsBackWhenSummaryPhaseNameEmpty,
still carries the pre-fix comment: "summary PhaseName stays empty when the
engine sees phases disabled, since GetSpendingPhaseNameAt returns \"\" in that
case too". Attempt 2's sentinel contract falsifies that sentence — the engine
now records "-" there. The test itself is a synthetic fixture that hard-codes
PhaseName: "" and still legitimately covers the legacy-projection path, so it
passes and is not wrong; only the comment documents dead behavior.

F-Z5-4 (test-coverage gap, V3-promotion candidate). No SHIPPED test composes
the engine and handler halves: the engine repro asserts summaries, the handler
repro hand-feeds a synthetic "-" summary. Consequently the handler repro
SURVIVES mutation M1 (engine identity) and the engine repro survives M2. My
throwaway probe covered the composed path and showed row y2="Slow-Go" leaking
under M1. Promoting that probe (real chain run -> buildSpendingTrajectoryRows)
would make the regression guard single-mutation-proof.

F-Z5-5 (serialization side-effect, informational). PhaseName carries
`json:"phase_name,omitempty"`, so disabled-phase years now serialize
`"phase_name":"-"` where they previously omitted the key entirely. Any
persisted/cached ProjectionResult JSON changes shape for those years. `make
check` is green (no golden-file breakage observed), and old cached projections
still deserialize to "" and hit the legacy fallback by design.
